(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-weight-control-quiz"]={"title":"Weight Control — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","questions":[{"q":"What is energy balance, per lecture's definition?","choices":["Energy intake equals energy output — food/beverage energy matches what's expended through basal metabolism and physical activity.","The relative balance maintained between an individual's total body fat mass and their total lean body mass over time.","The specific dietary ratio maintained between an individual's daily carbohydrate intake and daily fat intake.","The total number of discrete meals and snacks an individual chooses to eat across a single day."],"answer":0,"exp":"Energy balance means energy intake (food and beverages) matches energy output, primarily through basal metabolism and physical activity. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What results from positive energy balance?","choices":["Weight loss","Dehydration","No change in weight","Weight gain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Positive energy balance — intake greater than output — results in weight gain. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"About how many kcal does 1 pound of weight loss represent, per lecture?","choices":["500 kcal","1,000 kcal","7,000 kcal","3,500 kcal"],"answer":3,"exp":"1 pound of weight loss is approximately 3,500 kcal, per lecture. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does a bomb calorimeter measure, per lecture?","choices":["The calorific value of food and fuels, by measuring heat of combustion","Total body fat percentage using a skinfold-based estimation method","The rate of oxygen consumption during graded exercise testing","Fasting blood glucose levels drawn before a standard test meal"],"answer":0,"exp":"A bomb calorimeter measures the calorific value of food and fuels by measuring heats of combustion — burning food raises the temperature of water surrounding the chamber. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What percentage of total daily energy needs does basal metabolism (BMR) account for, per lecture?","choices":["8% to 15%","Nearly 100%","15% to 30%","60% to 80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Basal metabolism accounts for approximately 60% to 80% of total daily energy needs. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What percentage of total daily energy needs does physical activity typically account for, per lecture?","choices":["60% to 80%","50% to 60%","15% to 30%","8% to 15%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Physical activity accounts for approximately 15% to 30% of total daily energy needs. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What percentage of total daily energy needs does the thermic effect of food (TEF) account for, per lecture?","choices":["15% to 30%","40% to 50%","8% to 15%","60% to 80%"],"answer":2,"exp":"The thermic effect of food accounts for approximately 8% to 15% of total daily energy needs. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which macronutrient has the highest thermic effect of food (TEF), per lecture?","choices":["Carbohydrate","Protein","Fat","All three have an identical TEF."],"answer":1,"exp":"TEF is highest for protein, followed by carbohydrate, and then fat. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"If calorie intake is 3,000 kcal, roughly how many of those kcal does the thermic effect of food (TEF) account for, per lecture's example?","choices":["150 to 300 kcal","1,500 kcal","900 to 1,200 kcal","15 to 30 kcal"],"answer":0,"exp":"At 3,000 kcal intake, TEF (roughly 5-10% of intake) would account for about 150 to 300 kcal, per lecture's own worked example. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the single most significant contributor to basal metabolic rate, per lecture?","choices":["Chronological age alone","Total body weight","Dietary fat intake","Lean body mass (LBM)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Lean body mass (LBM) is described as the most significant contributor to basal metabolic rate, and it varies greatly between individuals of the same body weight. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is nonexercise activity thermogenesis (NEAT), per lecture?","choices":["A direct measurement of resting heart rate taken first thing each morning.","Energy burned through body posture, ambulation, and spontaneous movements like fidgeting.","Energy burned only during structured, intentional exercise sessions.","The extra energy cost of digesting and absorbing a single high-fat meal."],"answer":1,"exp":"NEAT is energy burned through body posture, ambulation, and other spontaneous movements including fidgeting — distinct from intentional exercise. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is adaptive thermogenesis, per lecture?","choices":["A term commonly used as a direct, interchangeable synonym for a person's fixed basal metabolic rate measured at complete rest.","A fixed, unchanging percentage of total daily calories burned no matter the diet, environmental temperature, or activity level.","The calories burned exclusively during deep sleep cycles each night, entirely unrelated to overeating or ambient temperature.","Nonvoluntary heat production triggered by overeating or environmental temperature changes, such as fidgeting, shivering, and maintaining muscle tone/posture."],"answer":3,"exp":"Adaptive thermogenesis is nonvoluntary physical activity triggered by overeating or environmental temperature change — fidgeting, shivering, and maintaining muscle tone and posture, involving brown adipose tissue. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does direct calorimetry measure, per lecture?","choices":["Oxygen intake and carbon dioxide output","Body heat output, using an insulated chamber","Body fat percentage via electrical resistance","Blood glucose response to a meal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Direct calorimetry measures body heat output using an insulated chamber — it's accurate but expensive and complex. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does indirect calorimetry measure, per lecture?","choices":["Skinfold thickness measured with calipers at multiple standardized body sites","Oxygen intake and carbon dioxide output, used to estimate energy expenditure","Waist circumference measured precisely at the level of the umbilicus","Body heat output directly, via an insulated chamber"],"answer":1,"exp":"Indirect calorimetry measures oxygen intake and carbon dioxide output as a surrogate for the substrates consumed and produced during metabolism, estimating energy expenditure. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as an indication that a person's weight is not healthy, per lecture?","choices":["Lower-body (gynoid) fat distribution only","A waist circumference below the cutoff for either sex","Normal blood glucose levels","Upper-body (android, \"apple-shaped\") fat distribution"],"answer":3,"exp":"Upper-body (android/apple-shaped) fat distribution is listed as an indication that weight is not healthy, along with hypertension, elevated non-HDL cholesterol, family history of obesity, and elevated blood glucose. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What BMI category does a BMI of 18.50-24.99 fall into, per the categories given?","choices":["Overweight (pre-obese)","Underweight","Obese class I","Normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"A BMI of 18.50-24.99 falls into the Normal category. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What BMI defines Obese Class III, the most severe obesity category, per lecture?","choices":["35.00-39.99","≥40.00","30.00-34.99","25.00-29.99"],"answer":1,"exp":"Obese Class III is defined as a BMI of 40.00 or greater. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What BMI range defines \"severe thinness\" within the underweight category, per lecture?","choices":["17.00-18.49","<16.00","16.00-16.99","18.50-24.99"],"answer":1,"exp":"Severe thinness is defined as a BMI below 16.00, the lowest of the three underweight subcategories. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What acceptable body fat percentage range is given for women, per lecture?","choices":["23% to 31%","13% to 21%","Over 35%","Under 10%"],"answer":0,"exp":"An acceptable body fat percentage for women is 23% to 31%. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What acceptable body fat percentage range is given for men, per lecture?","choices":["Over 25%","23% to 31%","Under 5%","13% to 21%"],"answer":3,"exp":"An acceptable body fat percentage for men is 13% to 21%. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which body-composition measurement method uses hydrostatic (underwater) weighing?","choices":["Skinfold measurement","Bioelectrical impedance","Dual X-ray photon absorptiometry (DXA)","Densitometry"],"answer":3,"exp":"Densitometry uses underwater (hydrostatic) weighing, which is very accurate — it relies on the fact that fat is less dense than lean tissue, using body weight and body volume to calculate body density. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How does the Bod Pod® measure body composition, per lecture?","choices":["By sending a small painless electrical current through the entire body","By pinching and measuring skinfold thickness at several sites with calipers","By determining body volume and air displaced in a sealed chamber (air displacement)","By performing a full-body dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry scan"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Bod Pod® uses air displacement — determining body volume and air displaced in a sealed chamber — to calculate body density (body weight per body volume). — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which body-fat measurement method is described as most accurate, but expensive, with minimal radiation dose?","choices":["Dual X-ray photon absorptiometry (DXA)","Skinfold measurement using handheld calipers","Underwater (hydrostatic) weighing","Bioelectrical impedance analysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"DXA is described as the most accurate method, though expensive, with a minimal radiation dose — the scanner arm moves from head to toe to determine body fat and bone density. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What waist circumference defines abdominal (android) obesity in men, per lecture?","choices":["Greater than 50 inches","Greater than 40 inches","Greater than 30 inches","Greater than 35 inches"],"answer":1,"exp":"A waist circumference greater than 40 inches defines abdominal (android) obesity in men. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What health risks are specifically associated with upper-body (android, \"apple-shaped\") fat distribution, per lecture?","choices":["No documented health risks whatsoever when compared to lower-body, gynoid fat distribution patterns.","Insulin resistance and fatty liver, with increased risk of cancer, type 2 diabetes, high blood lipids, and heart disease","A measurably reduced risk of developing type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular disease.","Improved whole-body insulin sensitivity, more favorable blood lipids, and lower cardiovascular risk."],"answer":1,"exp":"Android (upper-body/apple-shaped) obesity is associated with insulin resistance and fatty liver, and leads to obesity-related chronic diseases including cancer, type 2 diabetes, high blood lipids, and heart disease. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What hormones are described as encouraging lower-body (gynoid, \"pear-shaped\") fat distribution, per lecture?","choices":["Testosterone and growth hormone","Thyroid hormone and glucagon","Insulin and cortisol","Estrogen and progesterone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Estrogen and progesterone are described as encouraging gynoid (lower-body, \"pear-shaped\") fat distribution. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"According to lecture, what does research on identical twins suggest about body weight?","choices":["Identical twins tend to have similar weights even when raised apart, suggesting a genetic influence.","Identical twins consistently develop completely opposite BMI categories regardless of environment.","Identical twins tend to have very different weights even when raised together.","Twin studies show absolutely no measurable relationship between genetics and adult body weight."],"answer":0,"exp":"Identical twins tend to have similar weights even when raised apart, suggesting genes affect metabolic rate, fuel use, brain chemistry, and body weight. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the \"thrifty metabolism gene\" theory, per lecture?","choices":["A gene expressed exclusively in individuals with unusually low body fat percentage.","A gene associated with storing more fat to protect against famine.","A gene that completely eliminates the sensation of hunger.","A gene that increases metabolic rate in order to burn stored fat faster."],"answer":1,"exp":"The thrifty metabolism gene theory holds that more fat is stored to protect against famine — an evolutionary adaptation. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the set-point theory of weight regulation, per lecture?","choices":["The theory that a person's body weight is determined entirely by conscious willpower and discipline.","The theory of a genetically predetermined body weight, regulated by the hypothalamus, that the body resists changing.","The theory that weight always increases linearly with age, regardless of behavior.","The theory that body weight is randomly determined with no biological basis."],"answer":1,"exp":"Set-point theory proposes a genetically predetermined body weight, regulated by the hypothalamus, that the body resists changing — reducing calorie intake can lower metabolic rate in response. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Per lecture, how do the typical onset patterns of female vs. male obesity differ?","choices":["Female obesity is often related to childhood obesity; male obesity tends to appear after age 30.","Male obesity is often related to childhood obesity; female obesity tends to appear after age 30.","Neither sex shows any typical pattern of onset.","Both sexes show identical onset patterns tied only to menopause."],"answer":0,"exp":"Female obesity is often related to childhood obesity, while male obesity tends to appear after age 30, per lecture's discussion of environmental/nurture factors. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 38"}]};
